// PARAM: --analysis int --int-refinement once
// Int answers a check component by component, as Goblint's IntDomTuple
// does, so the mode reaches a check through what the stored states know. A
// join shows the difference.
//
// n is in [-100,100], so y = 4 * n + 1 cannot overflow i32 and is 1 (mod 4).
// On the true arm the guards bound y to [-2,10], so concretely y is 1, 5 or
// 9; the false arm sets y = 4, and y > 0 holds. Congruence knows the class,
// Interval the bounds. A refinement round moves the interval onto the class
// ([1,9]) before the join, which then keeps [1,9] and proves y > 0. Without
// refinement the join gives [-2,10] and no class (1 (mod 4) joined with 4),
// and no component decides the check.
//
// once and fixpoint agree here, and on every program we tried: each later
// operation refines again, which closes the gap one round leaves. That gap is
// visible on single values (Int_Refinement_Control.thy,
// refinement_once_not_fixpoint).
// Under once, the check is proved; under never it is not
// (../known-imprecision/05-refinement_never_keeps_guard_facts_apart.vimp).
fun main() {
  i32 n = __voblint_nondet_int();
  if (-100 <= n && n <= 100) {
    i32 y = 4 * n + 1;
    if (-2 <= y && y <= 10) {
      i32 x = 0;
    } else {
      y = 4;
    }
    __voblint_check(y > 0); // PROVED
  }
}
